    "title": "Globalization and culture: The three H scenarios",
    "cite": "Hassi and Storti (2012)",
    "kind": "Core reading",
    "reference": "Adapted from: Hassi, A., & Storti, G. (2012). Globalization and culture: The three H scenarios. In H. Cuadra-Montiel (Ed.), Globalization: Approaches to Diversity (pp. 3–20). IntechOpen. https://doi.org/10.5772/3332",
    "citeAs": "Cite as Hassi and Storti (2012) or (Hassi & Storti, 2012).",
    "paragraphs": [
      "A. Globalization is a multidimensional phenomenon that consists of economic, cultural, ideological and political components (Prasad & Prasad, 2006). However, the impacts of globalization on societies and their cultures are rarely discussed or debated. Indeed, any discussion or debate in this area is relatively recent (Acosta & Gonzalez, 2010). Moreover, the interaction between globalization and culture is an under-researched area (Prasad & Prasad, 2007). In a world where borders are becoming less relevant, maintaining cultural diversity is a controversial topic. This means opinions on the topic are contentious and conflicting. For instance, Cowen (2002) argues that, on the one hand, globalization causes changes and potential losses for local and traditional cultures. These include those with their own cultural differences. This may be damaging and destructive for those cultures. On the other hand, this may also lead to new opportunities. Therefore, it is argued that globalization can bring about diverse trends. These are cultural differentiation, cultural convergence and cultural hybridization (Pieterse, 1996). The latter two will be discussed in this article.",
      "## Cultural convergence: Homogenization scenario",
      "B. Globalization has led to a world rich with international exchanges and flows. These include humans, as well as goods, services, capital and technology transfer. This long and rich contact between different cultures has resulted in the phenomenon of acculturation. The debate around this is whether it is creating a more standardized and unique world culture. Perhaps it is resulting in a universal culture. The homogenization perspective seems to answer these questions in the positive. According to this perspective, the increased interconnection between countries and cultures contributes to forming a more homogenous world adopting the Western Euro-American lifestyle (Liebes, 2003). In the homogenization view, the barriers that stop cultures from becoming more alike are weak, while global influences are strong (Ritzer, 2010). In its extreme form, homogenization, which is also known as cultural convergence, suggests that local cultures can be shaped by other more powerful cultures. It also believes in the possibility of a global culture (Ritzer, 2010). This perspective is reflected in several concepts and models. These include the Global Culture, Americanization and more importantly the McDonaldization theory.",
      "C. Across different regions and countries in the world, more and more people seem to watch the same entertainment, listen to the same music, consume common global brand products and services, and wear the same or similar clothes (Prasad & Prasad, 2006). These comparable developments in cultural practices suggest that a “global culture” (Robertson, 1992) or a “world culture” (Meyer, Boli, Thomas & Ramirez, 1997) is emerging. In this way, globalization is helping to create a new group of people who share a global culture. This means that globalization is reducing the feeling of distance between countries, which used to make people feel more separate from other nations (Prasad & Prasad, 2006). Thus, globalization, which is a replication of the American and/or Western cultural tradition (Beck, 2000; Berger, 2002), is considered a destructive force, a recipe for cultural disaster (Jaja, 2010). It is an assault on local cultures, which they are not able to withstand or resist (Berger, 2002). This is presumably due to the fact that globalization contributes to weakening identities and destroying local cultural traditions and practices. This in turn is diluting the uniqueness of national cultures, and establishing a homogenized world culture.",
      "D. Jaja (2010) stresses that the world is presently experiencing Americanization, rather than globalization. Americanization refers to the global spread of America’s influential dominance and culture through drastic growth of mass communication and penetration of American companies in other countries. As a matter of fact, there seems to be an American hegemony reflected by its domination of the Internet. 85% of web pages originate from the United States, and American companies control 75% of the world’s packaged software market (Jaja, 2010). In addition to the latter, there is an American monopoly of the media as seen with popular films, music, and satellite and television stations around the world. It is important to note that the American lifestyle focuses on distributing cultural products widely, which boosts economic opportunities. This approach is attractive to both developed and developing countries.",
      "## Hybridization scenario",
      "E. Hybridization is another way to explain how globalization and culture interact with each other. It means mixing different cultural elements to create something new. This process is also known as translocal fusion and cultural mixing. According to the hybridization view, external and internal flows interact to create a unique cultural hybrid that encompasses components of both flows (Ritzer, 2010). Barriers to external flows exist. These barriers are powerful enough to protect local cultures from being overwhelmed. However, they are not powerful enough to completely block external flows. The main idea of cultural hybridization is the continuous process of mixing or blending cultures. This means that globalization brings together global and local ideas, creating new and unique cultures derived out of both (Cvetkovich & Kellner, 1997). These are distinctive and hybrid cultures. They are neither fully global nor fully local (Ritzer, 2010).",
      "F. Hybridization in cultural studies has therefore been associated with the idea of glocalization (Hannerz, 1987). Glocalization is the blending of global and local elements to create unique outcomes in different regions, and is central to hybridization (Giulianotti & Robertson, 2007). Glocalization shows that the world is becoming pluralistic, with individuals and communities becoming innovators that have power to adapt and innovate within their newly glocalized environment (Robertson, 2001).",
      "G. There is no doubt that cultures get influenced and shift through contact with other cultures. However, homogenization and hybridization focus on cultural products, not the deeper values or beliefs of a given culture. It is noteworthy to mention that the former do not impact the latter. In other words, the superficial elements of cultures such as clothing, fashion, foods, arts, music, movies and crafts get transferred but the deeper aspects remain tied to their original context and culture. Therefore, nations regularly get involved in cultural integration processes but do not lose the unique and intricate aspects of a culture that set it apart from others. They interpret cultural elements in light of their own in a way that they become compatible with their culture. In fact, cultural exchanges among nations are positive, as shown by the impact of global trade. These exchanges are not entirely destructive and negative for local cultures. In fact, they also bring about more possibilities and opportunities. In this regard, cultures are dynamic, not static. They can incorporate foreign elements without being necessarily subject to cultural domination."
    ]
