    "citeAs": "Cite as Van Krieken (2016) or (Van Krieken, 2016). A lecture produced by The University of Sydney for an Introduction to Sociology course.",
    "paragraphs": [
      "A. Good afternoon. Today, we will continue looking at GLOBALISATION. Globalisation has shaped the economic, political, legal and technological landscapes of societies and it has also had a huge impact on culture and how cultures interact with one another.",
      "B. Think about what you’ve done in the past 24 hours, what kind of food you’ve eaten, which TV shows or movies you’ve watched, who’s produced the music you’ve listened to, the slang that you’ve used in conversation. Chances are—a large proportion of the choices you make in everyday life are a direct result of globalisation. International travel, immigration and the internet mean that values, ideologies and forms of artistic expression and cultural practice are easily passed across national and cultural borders.",
      "C. In theory, these cultural flows go both ways. However, in practice this process has been criticised for being relatively one-sided. Critics of globalisation argue that trade between nations isn’t truly free and equal because of existing political and economic inequalities. The same applies to cultural exchanges. So, the critics say that the final result of globalisation is not a fair and equal cultural exchange, but cultural imperialism. Cultural imperialism refers to when a small minority of powerful political and corporate entities, mostly from the West, impose ideas, values and modes of cultural expression onto the rest of the world.",
      "D. Western nations and large international corporations are able to do this because they have the necessary economic means to do so. They have the money, so they produce the majority of the world’s cultural media. This includes advertising and entertainment. This then allows them to have significant cultural influence over other, less economically powerful countries. Critics of globalisation argue that Western nations export their cultural products such as movies, music, and fashion brands, and in doing so, they impose their own values and ideologies onto other nations. These values include political beliefs, legal principles, and ethical doctrines. They also include beauty standards and attitudes towards education, work, and health.",
      "E. Sociologist George Ritzer has looked at McDonald’s restaurants in his research on this topic. According to Ritzer, the presence of McDonald’s in almost every country in the world is significant because McDonald’s operational structure represents a specific type of social organisation. He says that McDonald’s promotes a bureaucratic approach to life that favours efficiency and control over creativity and imagination. So, there’s less or no focus on cultivating creativity and imagination. He says that they sell this way of thinking all over the world - with fries on the side.",
      "F. Just like McDonald’s, many of the most popular cultural products exported globally have their roots in the United States of America. In the context of globalisation, there is little doubt that America has been one of the key nations to export aspects of its culture to other parts of the world. Examples of American cultural imperialism or dominance include brand name products, mass-produced food and perhaps of primary importance—media and entertainment. Hollywood films and Coca-Cola don’t simply provide a product to sell to overseas markets. They sell a lifestyle and a specific way of seeing the world, which is ultimately grounded in American culture.",
      "G. It’s not surprising then that one of the most common critiques of globalisation is that it’s led to the Americanisation of the world. Proponents of this theory argue that globalisation is responsible for upholding rather than challenging American cultural hegemony—or the dominance of American cultural beliefs and values. This in turn leads to cultural homogenisation and standardisation, or we can say different cultures beginning to look similar—as people begin to give up a range of local cultural practices in favour of the dominant modes of Western expression. Popular American holidays like Halloween and Thanksgiving have also started to enter the culture of various countries around the world, as more people celebrate American holidays every year.",
      "H. However, Indian social anthropologist and sociologist Arjun Appadurai has a more optimistic view, contending that globalisation should not be thought of as cultural imperialism. He thinks people should also consider concepts like cultural hybridity. Cultural hybridity refers to the blending of elements from different cultures to create something new and unique. Appadurai suggests that all societies and cultures adopt aspects of globalisation in different ways and at different speeds. So, rather than having a unifying impact on them, globalisation manifests itself differently in every society.",
      "I. In Appadurai’s thesis, media and migration are the most important factors to influence the global cultural changes we’re seeing today. However, the impact of these factors on nations depends on a number of other considerations, such as the political stability of the state, the strength of the national economy, and the durability of existing cultural traditions. In other words, he doesn’t deny that globalisation affects and shapes cultures, but he also doesn’t believe that the result is an Americanisation of all cultures. Appadurai argues instead that cultural identities are becoming less constrained by geographical space and nationality, and as a result, new cultural identities that have elements from different cultures are emerging. This leads to greater cultural heterogeneity, in some respects, and homogeneity in others.",
      "J. Clearly, the impact of globalisation on culture is both unifying and diversifying, depending on the local contexts and the ways in which global influences are adapted and reshaped by local cultures."
    ]
