      "## Essay question: Is cultural globalisation having a negative impact on culture? (Write 450–600 words)",
      "¹In today’s modern world, advancements in technology and increased international travel have led to a more interconnected globalised world, in which some ideas, values and behaviour transcend national borders (Van Krieken, 2016). ²While interconnection has spurred economic growth and global collaboration, it also raises significant concerns about the impact on cultural identities. ³This essay asserts that cultural globalisation predominantly exerts a negative influence on traditional cultures. ⁴This is because it has caused an overwhelming prevalence of American values which has led to a loss of cultural uniqueness and despite some multi-directional cultural exchange, this tends to be uneven.",
      "¹In today’s globalised world, it is specifically an American influence which is dominating the global culture. ²In a phenomenon that both Hassi and Storti (2012) and Van Krieken (2016) refer to as “Americanisation” and “American hegemony,” the United States is monopolising global entertainment, media and fashion industries, with American brands such as McDonalds and Coca-Cola becoming omnipresent around the world. ³Consequently, American values and standards are being imposed on the consumers of these products and media, thus undermining their own traditions (Van Krieken, 2016). ⁴Even if Americanisation is becoming less of a concern in some developing areas with growing global profiles and influence, like some Asian countries (Crysell et al., 2023), other less developed regions are probably still being left behind in this regard.",
      "¹As a result of this globalised, Americanised world, traditional cultures can lose their unique identity. ²Through the global connectedness which the internet, international travel and migration have facilitated, some local identities are being replaced by a global culture in which the same brands, fashion, music and entertainment are popular worldwide (Hassi & Storti, 2012; Van Krieken, 2016). ³As a result, in many countries, for instance in Asia, young people are torn between maintaining cultural pride and their desire to be global citizens (Crysell et al., 2023). ⁴For many, the pull towards a global culture is too strong to resist (Hassi & Storti, 2012), which contributes to a homogenised world in which the resemblance between different cultures is growing closer. ⁵Ultimately, this cultural homogenisation can lead to the erosion of unique cultural elements and traditions (Hassi & Storti, 2012; Van Krieken, 2016).",
      "¹On the other hand, many argue that globalisation can be seen to facilitate diversity and innovation by blending different cultures together. ²Some scholars argue that the multi-directional cultural exchange of globalisation creates a diverse global landscape of societies which blend external cultural artifacts with traditional practices in unique ways (Hassi & Storti, 2012; Crysell et al., 2023; Van Krieken, 2016). ³However, this exchange is ultimately unbalanced, and countries that are not wealthy or politically stable are more likely to adopt outside influence (Van Krieken, 2016), making them more vulnerable to culture loss.",
      "¹In summary, this essay has argued that cultural globalisation has negative impacts on culture as the dominance of American cultural norms and values creates a homogenised global culture that often comes at the expense of local traditions. ²Despite the potential for cultural exchange and innovation, this is often disproportionately balanced, leaving economically and politically weaker nations more vulnerable to cultural erosion. ³It is imperative that international policies and practices evolve to protect and nurture the world’s diverse cultural traditions to prevent further cultural loss in the future."
